    usage: echo [-n] [ARG ...]
    Print the ARGs separated by spaces and a newline; -n as the first ARG omits the newline.
    Backslashes are printed as they are: printf interprets escapes.
FILE /usr/share/man/cat1/test.1
    usage: test EXPRESSION
           [ EXPRESSION ]
    Exit 0 when EXPRESSION is true, 1 when it is false or missing, 2 when it is malformed.
      -e FILE          FILE exists (-r and -w are the same: there are no permission bits)
      -f FILE          FILE is a regular file
      -d FILE          FILE is a directory
      -x FILE          FILE is a regular file or a directory
      -h FILE, -L FILE FILE is a symbolic link
      -s FILE          FILE is not empty
      -b, -c, -p, -S FILE  FILE is a block or character device, a FIFO, a socket
      -g, -u, -k FILE  the set-group-ID, set-user-ID, sticky bit of the fixed modes stat reports
      -t FD            descriptor FD is a terminal
      -n STRING        STRING is not empty (so is a lone STRING)
      -z STRING        STRING is empty
      A = B, A != B    the strings are equal (== is the same), differ
      A < B, A > B     A sorts before, after B
      M -eq N          the integers are equal; also -ne -lt -le -gt -ge
      F -nt G, F -ot G F was modified after, before G (a missing file is older)
      F -ef G          F and G are the same file
      ! E, E -a E, E -o E, ( E )   not, and, or, grouping; up to four arguments
      count, as POSIX says, so test "$x" = -a compares strings
    example: test -d /tmp && [ "$HOME" != / ]
